the world of the medium begs to be  closely examined to be parsed for those  small details that begin to paint  monsters as something all too familiar  there's something disturbing about the  reminder of the evils that humans can  inflict on one another and how horrific  acts of abuse rarely if  ever result in a singular trauma the  after effects of such actions can fester  in the heart and mind of victims long  after and it's here that the medium  finds the basis for its story  one that concludes as a memorable  experience  in the medium you play as marianne a  powerful clairvoyant who travels to the  neva resort she goes there in search of  thomas a man who leaves her a strange  message telling her to find and help him  promising that he'll give her the answer  she seeks about her  past in return marianne is able to  commune with spirits and help them pass  on to the afterlife to that end the  medium plays out on two planes of  existence  the normal world and the spirit world  these two depictions of the world are  not black and white opposites instead  the game posits that they exist as  mirrors of one another  one manifesting literally with the other  only hints at figuratively  and via this shared window into both  perspectives the medium is able to  explore the trauma of its characters  through puzzle solving and riddles as  you explore the neva resort you'll  stumble into areas where the emotional  remnants of the world are so powerful  myrian is forced to traverse the space  in both the real  and spiritual plane at this point your  perspective is split forcing you to look  at and explore both worlds at once  navigating these spaces isn't  straightforward though obstacles in one  reality can halt your progress  in both these moments of dual reality  transform areas into self-contained  puzzle boxes where an understanding of  the space is necessary to move forward  as you continue the puzzles become more  elaborate  encouraging you to take an even closer  look at the environment in search of  clues the puzzles revolving around the  dual reality of marion's life for the  best part of the game  impressive in their technical  achievement as they are two separate  experiences  running side by side but beyond how cool  it is to see xbox's new hardware finally  get an  exclusive that accomplishes something  that feels very next-gen  these moments of dual reality also add  to the medium's gameplay myrian is able  to have an out-of-body experience  allowing you to control  only her spirit form for a limited time  god i hate this  so in order to solve certain puzzles you  may need marianne to be in two places at  once  regardless of whether they're a part of  the dual reality sections the puzzles  are satisfying to solve  most puzzles require a level of spatial  awareness or deductive reasoning they're  less about leaving you stumped and  geared more towards smartly navigating  spaces  as well you can't guess your way to  success figuring out what to do  shouldn't trip you up for very long  there's a great deal of incentive in  solving each puzzle prior to  arrival the naval resort suffered a  horrifying incident in which almost  everyone on the premises died  but no one knows why journeying deeper  into the resort begins to fill in those  gaps unveiling a mystery that compels  you to push further through the horror  in hopes of finding the answers you seek  the medium has stealth encounters and  chase sequences as well  both of these stem from encounters with  the maw the main antagonists of the game  the mod is an unsettling foe made all  the more so because you go a great deal  of the game not knowing  why it wants to be inside you but  knowing that it can appear at almost  any time thankfully there are a few  systems designed to ensure you don't  always outright fail if you make a  mistake during a chase or stealth  section  when captured provided you have spirit  energy you can emit a blast that  temporarily stuns the moth  allowing you to keep running or flee to  a hiding spot miriam can't hold an  endless supply of spirit energy but you  usually always have enough to escape at  least once  so the first time you're caught it's not  necessarily a game over  of course that doesn't dispel the  unnerving atmosphere when the maw is  nearby  stay with me  that feeling of unease is only  heightened by how you're able to  perceive the medium's world  bloomer team strips you of any agency  over the camera opting for a more  cinematic experience where at the mercy  of predetermined angles for every room  often you're forced to view a room from  a less than ideal perspective to the  game's credit  it's very effective for establishing the  tone of every moment allowing bluebird  team to orchestrate the same type of  shot composition seen in  well-choreographed films  or cinematic horror games like silent  hill the medium does a terrific job of  storytelling without explicitly telling  you what's going on so it's  disappointing when it goes out of its  way to explicitly lay things out  almost the entire game is a flashback of  past events as marianne recaps her  journey through the neva resort  narrating her innermost thoughts  feelings and convictions during each  moment  she's excessively vocal often explaining  revelations that do not need explaining  because the game has already  sufficiently established a situation  i had never felt anything so  desperate  it was calling out to me wanted to show  me something  yeah kelly burke's stellar performance  does much of the work in conveying  marianne's mental and emotional state so  getting narration about how she's  feeling just after inferring it from the  tone in her voice feels  redundant the medium gives you  everything you need to piece together  its puzzles and narrative but whereas it  leaves you to solve the puzzles on your  own  it goes too far in trying to ensure you  never miss a beat of its story  the medium's initial hook of finding  thomas in order to learn about marion's  origins isn't all that interesting but  as mentioned before the walls of neva  resort hide many compelling secrets  early on you discover evidence of  several seemingly unimportant but  fascinating stories that speak to what  transpired in the days  leading up to the massacre the left neva  resort abandoned many of these stories  tie into the puzzles more often than not  deducing the identity of someone and  understanding who they were is key to  moving forward thus to understand the  puzzle in front of you  you need to empathize with those who  once lived there to figure out how they  ticked even if you can't fully  comprehend the scope of their trauma  it's important to acknowledge it and try  to understand how it may have shaped  them  two moments in particular stand out once  we are entering the minds of victims  that went on to become victimizers  themselves  these sections play out in unique  dreamscapes inspired by the twisted  subconscious mind of the individuals in  question  they're both harrowing and intense  you're cutting away at the defenses that  someone has erected against the most  traumatic moments of their life  but they're important because they  clarify the thematic core of the  medium's message about only judging  someone after knowing both sides of the  story  in both cases marianne outright rejects  the use of an abusive upbringing to  forgive evil actions  you can feel bad for a villain for what  happened to them but that doesn't mean  you should ever forgive what they did  it's an important distinction and one  i'm glad the game makes and i think  therein lies why the medium works as  well as it does it's a horror game no  doubt about that but it also feels like  a game about the aftermath of horror and  how living with unresolved trauma can  create nightmares as damaging as the  initial event as scary as it is to be  chased by a monster it's far more  terrifying to see how one traumatizing  act  can have such a ripple effect on the  victim than those around them eventually  the medium's narrative threads begin to  weave together and the promise that  everything somehow connects in some  unforeseen way is a thrilling reason to  see the game all the way through  the references to real world events of  polish history make many of the  revelations hit that much harder  even if the supernatural aspects are  fictional it's deeply unsettling to  think about how some of the evil present  in this game  did actually occur and to that end the  medium's conclusion sticks with you it's  one last horrifying reminder that trauma  may not define a person but it can have  consequences we can't even begin to  foresee if it's not adequately dealt  with  i can't say i enjoyed the ending to the  medium but i am deeply satisfied with  how it sets up the inescapable nature of  its horrifying conclusion  you
 

